content.text:
I reviewed `app/importer.py` against the ticket's own stated contract ("rows that can't be imported get reported back to the admin, and the rest of the file still imports") and against how `csv`-producing systems ("HR exports, spreadsheets, whatever they've got") actually behave. I reproduced each finding with a script rather than just reading the code.

```json
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "unhandled-csv-error-aborts-whole-file",
      "title": "Malformed CSV input crashes the entire import instead of failing one row",
      "requirement": "Ticket: 'Rows that can't be imported get reported back to the admin, and the rest of the file still imports' — this must hold for any file the customer's own systems can produce, since 'we don't control how they're produced'",
      "evidence": "app/importer.py wraps nothing around `next(reader)` or the `for row in reader` loop. Python's csv module raises `_csv.Error` for classic-Mac bare-`\\r` line endings ('new-line character seen in unquoted field') and for any single field over the default 128KB field_size_limit ('field larger than field limit'). Reproduced: import_users('email,name,team\\rada@example.com,...\\r') raises _csv.Error uncaught; import_users with one 200KB field also raises _csv.Error uncaught.",
      "failure": "A single stray `\\r`-only line ending (common from older HR/spreadsheet export tools) or one oversized free-text field (e.g. a long 'notes' column) anywhere in a 340-row file causes the admin upload view to 500 on the whole request — zero rows import and the admin gets a stack trace instead of a report, even though every other row in the file was fine.",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "line-number-misattribution-with-embedded-newlines",
      "title": "Reported error row numbers are wrong when an earlier row has a multi-line quoted field",
      "requirement": "Ticket: rows that fail must be 'reported back to the admin' — the report is only actionable if it identifies the correct row",
      "evidence": "app/importer.py:85 uses `reader.line_num`, which counts raw physical text lines, not logical CSV records. Reproduced: with a valid row containing an embedded newline in a quoted field (e.g. a multi-line 'notes' column) followed by a row with a bad email, the error is reported as 'row 4' when the actual data row is the 3rd row in the file (as any spreadsheet app or line-numbered view would count it).",
      "failure": "An admin trying to locate and fix 'row 4' in their spreadsheet/text editor finds the wrong row or no row at all, since any legitimate multi-line field earlier in the file shifts every subsequent reported line number out of sync with the file's actual record order.",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ]
}
```

